# FCLOSE(3S)

## NAME

fclose, fflush - close or flush a stream

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int fclose(FILE *stream);

int fflush(FILE *stream);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Fclose* causes any buffers for the named *stream* to be emptied, and the file to be closed.
Buffers allocated by the standard input/output system are freed.

*Fclose* is performed automatically upon calling exit(3). **Note:** v7's page said exit(2). It
is exit(3) that does it - _exit(2) flushes nothing, which is the whole difference between the
two.

*Fflush* causes any buffered data for the named output *stream* to be written to that file. The
stream remains open.

**Note:** a null *stream* flushes every open output stream. That is C11 sec 7.21.5.2; v7 had no
such case.

## SEE ALSO

close(2), fopen(3), setbuf(3), exit(3), stdio(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

These routines return **EOF** if *stream* is not associated with an output file, or if buffered
data cannot be transferred to that file.

## BESM-6 NOTES

A line-buffered stream is held at **_cnt == 0** (see stdio(3)), and *fflush* must **leave it
there.** Restoring a real count would send the next *putc* down the fast path and lose the
newline flush.

The cleanup that exit(3) runs is reached through a *pointer* that this file's *_flsbuf*() arms
on the first buffered write, never by name. Naming it would put the whole of stdio into every
program ever linked, because *crt0* tail-jumps to *exit*: the **hello** program measured 2255
words that way and 100 through the pointer. See exit(3).

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/stdio/flsbuf.c - fflush, fclose and the cleanup share one file, as v7 keeps them
